      "message": "IIUC, this introduces kind of an artificial node into the dependency graph so keep the objects from vkUpdateDescriptorSets alive.\n\nSo if you do \n\n```\nVkDescriptorSets descriptorSets[2];\n\nvkAllocateDescriptorSets( ... { 2 }... \u0026descriptorSets);\n\n// descriptorSets \u003d { VkDescriptorSet1, VkDescriptorSet2 };\n\nvkUpdateDescriptorSets({ \n   VkDescriptorSet1 -\u003e VkImage1,\n   VkDescriptorSet2 -\u003e VkBuffer1,\n});\n```\n\nthere is effectively a tree:\n\n```\n\u003csynthetic handle\u003e\n   -\u003e \u003cApiInfo for vkUpdateDescriptorSets\u003e\n   -\u003e VkDescriptorSet1 \n     -\u003e \u003cApiInfo for vkAllocateDescriptorSets()\u003e\n     -\u003e VkImage1\n       -\u003e \u003cApiInfo for vkCreateImage()\u003e\n   -\u003e VkDescriptorSet2\n     -\u003e \u003cApiInfo for vkAllocateDescriptorSets()\u003e\n     -\u003e VkBuffer1\n       -\u003e \u003cApiInfo for vkCreateBuffer()\u003e\n```\n\n2 questions:\n\n1) IIUC, because the synthetic handle created by `newGlobalVkGenericHandle()` is not associated with anything, it never gets deleted via `removeHandles()` nor overwritten. I believe this means that Gfxstream would end up with an infinetly growing snapshot when opening a simple Vulkan app that updates a descriptor set every frame to render a triangle. I think somehow, we would have to tie the lifetime of the synthetic handle to the lifetime of the descriptor sets - maybe this needs a `std::shared_ptr\u003cuint64_t\u003e handle` that is shared between all of the descriptor sets in the `vkUpdateDescriptorSets()` call and then subsequent calls to `vkUpdateDescriptorSets()` associates the latest handle to the descriptor set?\n\n2) Is it correct that this does not address the case of \n\n```\nVkDescriptorSets descriptorSets[2];\n\nvkAllocateDescriptorSets( ... { 2 }... \u0026descriptorSets);\n\n// descriptorSets \u003d { VkDescriptorSet1, VkDescriptorSet2 };\n\nvkUpdateDescriptorSets({ \n   VkDescriptorSet1 -\u003e VkImage1,\n   VkDescriptorSet2 -\u003e VkBuffer1,\n});\n\nvkFreeDescriptorSet( ... VkDescriptorSet1 ... );\n```",
